# DEFECT 089: a C library that ignores SIGABRT and then raises it goes on, and
# the runtime killed it (found at panel 175's landing, 2026-09-23).
#
# Panel 173's install took SIGTRAP, SIGABRT and SIGILL whatever it found, so a
# signal the program had set to be ignored reached the lease handler instead:
# exit 134 under `the process died with 1 lease(s) still live`, measured on the
# trunk at `a747e5a2`, for a program whose right answer is to print and exit 0.
# The handler changed what the process does, which is the one thing its own
# comment says it never does. An ignored signal is now put back and not taken,
# on the POSIX arms and on Windows' `signal`.

extern "fixedbugs-an-ignored-signal-stays-ignored.h"
    function raise_ignored() -> i32

function main()
    word = repeat("ab", 4)
    held: cstr @= word.lease()
    print("ignored: ", raise_ignored())
    end_lease(@held)
